Fix: 5.1 XMA sounds lost their centre / rear channels after the first buffer

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk),
            on top of 0001 (same file)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0007-xma-multistream-buffer-carry.patch
Status:     local patch, not upstream yet (worth reporting; Xenia has the
            same code)

WHAT BROKE
  In Crash: Mind over Mutant's NV cutscene (internal name nvmail: a box of
  NVs arrives at Crash's house on Wumpa Island), Coco's line "Oh my god, somebody sent us NVs!" was
  never heard: the scene's dialogue stopped after about 3.4 seconds and the
  rest of the scene was silent, although the game kept playing it.

WHY
  Cutscene dialogue is a 5.1 XMA sound. On the Xbox 360 a 5.1 XMA sound is
  THREE stereo streams (front L/R, centre + LFE, rear L/R), each decoded by
  its own XMA context, all reading the same input buffers: the streams'
  2 KB packets are interleaved, and each packet's "skip count" says how many
  packets to jump to reach that stream's next one. Every buffer (64 KB
  blocks here) starts with one packet per stream, in stream order, so the
  jump at the end of a buffer lands INSIDE the next one: stream 1 goes from
  packet 30 of a 32-packet buffer to packet 1 of the next.
  XmaContext::Decode (and GetNextPacket, for a frame split across the
  boundary) always restarted at the next buffer's packet 0. For stream 0
  that is right; streams 1 and 2 went on decoding stream 0's packets. The
  dialogue sits in the centre channel (stream 1), so from the second buffer
  on the centre channel played the front channels' near-silence.
  Single-stream sounds (mono / stereo: all music, effects, in-game voices)
  always jump to packet 0 and never saw it.

THE FIX (src/audio/xma_context.cpp)
  1. GetNextPacket: a packet index past the end of the current buffer is
     counted into the next buffer (index - packet count), not packet 0.
  2. End of Decode: when the stream continues in the next buffer, start at
     that same carried packet (its first frame); if the next buffer isn't
     valid yet, point the read offset at that packet anyway.
  A carry of 0 (every single-stream sound) behaves exactly as before.

HOW WE FOUND IT (docs/findings/19-missing-dialogue-5-1-xma.md)
  A new log of every sound the game asks for (--debug_audio_trace) named
  the scene's dialogue track (nis\6ch\l1_r1_sc4_nvmail_ch*.wav, 3 streams,
  11.04 s); decoded from the disc with ffmpeg it is all centre channel,
  speech until 9.7 s. A recording of the game's own output (the new
  --debug_audio_dump) had the centre channel go silent at 3.4 s, exactly
  where the file's first 64 KB block ends (sample 161,792), and the packet
  headers showed each stream crossing into the next block at packet 0 / 1 /
  2. After the fix the output's centre channel follows the disc decode for
  all 11 s (20 ms loudness curves correlate 1.000).

--- a/src/audio/xma_context.cpp
+++ b/src/audio/xma_context.cpp
@@ -320,7 +320,16 @@
     return nullptr;
   }
 
-  return memory()->TranslatePhysical(next_buffer_address);
+  // The packet is in the NEXT buffer, as many packets in as the skip count
+  // reached past the end of this one. In a multi-stream sound (5.1 = three
+  // stereo streams sharing each buffer) that is not its first packet, which
+  // belongs to stream 0 (see the end of Decode). Single-stream sounds always
+  // land on packet 0 here, as before.
+  const uint32_t next_buffer_packet = next_packet_index - current_input_packet_count;
+  if (next_buffer_packet >= data->GetInputBufferPacketCount(next_buffer_index)) {
+    return nullptr;
+  }
+  return memory()->TranslatePhysical(next_buffer_address) + next_buffer_packet * kBytesPerPacket;
 }
 
 uint32_t XmaContext::GetNextPacketReadOffset(uint8_t* buffer, uint32_t next_packet_index,
@@ -782,15 +791,36 @@
       GetNextPacketReadOffset(current_input_buffer, next_packet_index, current_input_packet_count);
 
   if (next_input_offset == kBitsPerPacketHeader) {
+    // This stream continues in the next buffer, at the packet its skip count
+    // points past the end of this one. A multi-stream sound (5.1 XMA = three
+    // stereo streams, each on its own context) interleaves the streams'
+    // packets in every buffer, each buffer starting with one packet per
+    // stream in stream order: stream 1 (centre + LFE) goes from packet 30 of
+    // a 32-packet buffer to packet 1 of the next. Restarting every stream at
+    // packet 0 made streams 1 and 2 play stream 0's packets from the second
+    // buffer on: a cutscene's dialogue (centre channel) went silent after its
+    // first 64 KB block (Crash: Mind over Mutant, the NV-mail scene).
+    // Single-stream sounds always land on packet 0 (carry 0), as before.
+    const uint32_t carry = next_packet_index > current_input_packet_count
+                               ? next_packet_index - current_input_packet_count
+                               : 0;
     SwapInputBuffer(data);
     if (data->IsAnyInputBufferValid()) {
-      next_input_offset = xma::GetPacketFrameOffset(
-          memory()->TranslatePhysical(data->GetCurrentInputBufferAddress()));
+      const uint32_t packet_count = data->GetCurrentInputBufferPacketCount();
+      const uint32_t packet = carry < packet_count ? carry : 0;
+      const uint32_t frame_offset = xma::GetPacketFrameOffset(
+          memory()->TranslatePhysical(data->GetCurrentInputBufferAddress()) +
+          packet * kBytesPerPacket);
 
-      if (next_input_offset > kMaxFrameSizeinBits) {
+      if (frame_offset > kMaxFrameSizeinBits) {
         SwapInputBuffer(data);
         return;
       }
+      next_input_offset = packet * kBitsPerPacket + frame_offset;
+    } else {
+      // The next buffer isn't there yet: point at the packet anyway (Decode
+      // moves to its first frame once the buffer is valid).
+      next_input_offset = carry * kBitsPerPacket + kBitsPerPacketHeader;
     }
   }
   data->input_buffer_read_offset = next_input_offset;
